Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillet: of two roundings whose blends overlap on a face narrower than
both, the later one's ball rolls tangent on the earlier one's blend
surface, the earlier blend cut by its contact there, the face vanishing
between them; for roundings of either kind (mitcad#1926).

A wall 2.5 wide whose top's edges are rounded with 2 and 1, a plate 2.5
wide behind a tower whose foot is rounded with 2 (concave) and whose back
edge is rounded with 1 (convex), a step 1.45 high whose foot is rounded
with 1 and whose top's edge with 0.5: the face between the two edges is
narrower than the two roundings together. The shape the two roundings
make one after the other has the smaller ball tangent to its other face
and to the larger rounding's blend, which its contact there cuts; the
face between goes. OCCT's fillet computed the two blends overflowing that
face (0013) and failed, and then, for roundings of one kind, rolled the
later one on the earlier one's contact line on the face (0016): another
shape (on the wall, 4.5 mm3 more material). Of the other kind it failed.

- ChFi3d_Builder::RideOnBlend (from Compute, for each pair of stripes
  overlapping on a face before they are computed overflowing it, whose
  success depended on the stripes' order; and before RideOnOverlap for a
  pair the overflow failed for): where the two are constant roundings of
  the other kind, or of one kind with the later one the smaller, both
  open contours whose ends meet no other stripe: the earlier one (the
  larger) is computed on the faces of its edge; a face is made of its
  blend surface over its contacts' parameters, run on a quarter past its
  ends along its contact, oriented as the face between where the blend
  touches it (the support); the later one is computed between its other
  face and the support (StartSolOverflow: its first section from the one
  on the faces of its edge, its contact on the face between projected
  onto the support; no analytic blend). Each end of the earlier one's
  contact on the face between lies on an arc of it; the later one's
  contact crosses the other face of that arc (the face at the end) there.
  The later one's side on the support lies on the face between, its curve
  projected there (as a ride on a contact line, 0016); the earlier one's
  contact on that face becomes the later one's contact on its blend (its
  p-curve there the one on the support), and both stripes' points at
  those ends are mere points on that curve.
- PerformOneCorner: at an end of a stripe of such a ride whose point on
  the face between is a mere point, the face at the end is the other
  face of the arc of its other side's point (not OnSame); the cap is the
  blend's intersection with it between that point on the arc and the mere
  point (its point on the face at the end projected), oriented from that
  arc.
- ApplyRides then makes the two blends meet on that curve (each side on
  its own surface, on no face), cuts the face between's other edges at
  their vertices and makes the stripes' points there one, as for 0016.
  The stripes go into the data structure (ChFi3d_FilDS) with the riders
  after the others, so that the line two blends share is put in by the
  stripe ridden on first, as for 0016's riders, which come later anyway
  (a rider whose edge was given first left that line reversed and the
  rider's face invalid).
- ChFi3d_Builder holds the tangent riders (myTangentRides; IsTangentRide,
  IsTangentRideOn), with RideOf's lists.
- Where the two edges' spines run opposite ways round the face between,
  the earlier stripe holds the later one's contact reversed, a curve of
  its own, and the later one rides against it (as 0016's riders against a
  line, turnRidesAgainst).

Rides that end at corners of more blends (a stripe's end vertex shared
with another stripe) and part rides (the face between narrower along a
part of the edges only, the contacts crossing part of the way) are not
made so: the roundings are computed as before.

Test: geometry/tests/test_fillet_tangent_ride.cpp (ctest
geometry.fillet_tangent_ride): test_wall (2.9, 2.5 and 2.127 wide),
test_tower_foot (2.9, 2.5 and 2.2) and test_step (1.45 and 1.3 high), each
in both orders: valid, the volume of the section within a millionth;
without the patch the wall's result has more material and the other two
fail. It fails with this patch and 9 later patches that change the same
lines reverted together, so the test does not single this patch out (shown
2026-11-20 by tools/occt-port-proof.py; the patches are listed in
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-before-after.md).

Failures (mitcad#1986): the two stripes' computations again for the ride
fail with OCCT's reports of a computation it cannot make
(ChFi3d_IsComputationFailure, 0016): the ride fails, as 0016's. Any other
failure (a broken invariant, a signal) is rethrown.
